from math12_reviewed_unit1 import heading,paragraph,page,command
def eq(s,n=None):return command(None,'\\('+s+'\\)',n)
def note(s):return {'type':'clarification','text':s}
PAGES=[page('055_page_1.jpg',101,[
 heading('Introduction'),paragraph(r'The higher order derivatives has useful physical interpretation. If y = f(t) is the position of an object at time t then \(\frac{dy}{dt}=f′(t)\) is its velocity at time t and \(\frac{d^2y}{dt^2}\) is its acceleration at time t.'),
 paragraph('According to the Newton’s law of motion “The acceleration of an object is proportional to the total force acting on it”. So, the second order derivatives has importance in mechanics. The second order derivatives is also important to graph the functions. Now, in this unit we will learn in details about higher order differentiation and its applications.'),
 heading('4.1 Higher order derivatives'),paragraph('If a function y = f(x) has a first derivative y′, then the derivative of y′, if it exists, is the second derivative of y = f(x), written as y″. The derivative of y″, if it exists, is called the third derivative of y = f(x), written as y‴. By continuing this process, we can find fourth derivative and other higher derivatives.'),
 paragraph(r'For example, if \(f(x)=x^4+2x^3+3x^2-5x+7\), then the higher derivatives are the following:'),
 eq(r'y′=f′(x)=\frac{dy}{dx}=4x^3+6x^2+6x-5\quad\text{first derivative of }y'),
 eq(r'y″=f″(x)=\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\frac{d}{dx}(\frac{dy}{dx})=12x^2+12x+6\quad\text{second derivative of }y'),
 eq(r'y‴=f‴(x)=\frac{d^3y}{dx^3}=\frac{d}{dx}(\frac{d^2y}{dx^2})=24x+12\quad\text{third derivative of }y')
]),page('055_page_2.jpg',102,[
 heading('Example 1'),paragraph(r'Find the second derivative of the following functions: (a) \(f(x)=8x^3-9x^2+6x+4\); (b) \(f(x)=\frac{4x+2}{3x-1}\).'),heading('Solution'),
 paragraph(r'a. If the given function is \(f(x)=8x^3-9x^2+6x+4\), then the first and second derivatives of the given function through linearity property are the following:'),
 eq(r'f′(x)=\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{d}{dx}(8x^3-9x^2+6x+4)=24x^2-18x+6'),eq(r'f″(x)=\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\frac{d}{dx}(24x^2-18x+6)=48x-18'),
 heading('Remember'),paragraph('The second derivative of y = f(x) can be written with any of the following notations:'),eq(r'\frac{d^2y}{dx^2},\quad y″,\quad f″,\quad f″(x),\quad D^2[f(x)]'),paragraph('The third derivative can be written in a similar way. For n ≥ 4, the derivative holds the notation f⁽ⁿ⁾(x), n = 4,5,...'),
 paragraph(r'b. If the given function is \(f(x)=y=\frac uv=\frac{4x+2}{3x-1}\), then the first and second derivatives of the given function through quotient rule are the following:'),
 eq(r'\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{d}{dx}(\frac{4x+2}{3x-1})=\frac{(3x-1)\frac{d}{dx}(4x+2)-(4x+2)\frac{d}{dx}(3x-1)}{(3x-1)^2}=\frac{4(3x-1)-3(4x+2)}{(3x-1)^2}=\frac{-10}{(3x-1)^2}'),
 eq(r'\frac{d}{dx}(\frac uv)=\frac{v\frac{du}{dx}-u\frac{dv}{dx}}{v^2}'),
 eq(r'f″(x)=\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\frac{d}{dx}(\frac{dy}{dx})=\frac{0(3x-1)^2-(-10)(2)(3x-1)(3)}{(3x-1)^4}=\frac{60(3x-1)}{(3x-1)^4}=\frac{60}{(3x-1)^3}'),
 paragraph('In the previous unit, we saw that the first derivative of a function represents the rate of change of the function. The second derivative, then, represents the rate of change of the first derivative. If a function describes the position of a moving object at time t, then the first derivative gives the velocity of the object. That is, if y = s(t) describes the position of the object at time t, then v(t) = s′(t) gives the velocity at a time t.'),
 paragraph('The rate of change of velocity is called acceleration. Since the second derivative gives the rate of change of the first derivative, the acceleration is the derivative of the velocity. Thus, if a(t) represents the acceleration at time t, then'),eq(r'a(t)=\frac{dv}{dt}=\frac{d^2s}{dt^2}=s″(t)'),
 heading('Example 2'),paragraph(r'An object is moving along a straight line with its position s(t) (in feet) at time t (in seconds): \(s(t)=t^3-2t^2-7t+9\). (a) Find the velocity at any time t. (b) Find the acceleration at any time t. (c) The object stops when velocity is zero. For t ≥ 0, when does that occur?'),heading('Solution'),
 paragraph('a. The velocity at any time t is the first derivative of s(t) w.r.t. t:'),eq(r'v=\frac{ds}{dt}=3t^2-4t-7'),paragraph('b. The acceleration at any time t is the first derivative of v(t) w.r.t. t:'),eq(r'a=\frac{dv}{dt}=6t-4'),paragraph('c. Use v(t) = 0 to obtain the time:'),eq(r'3t^2-4t-7=0,\quad(3t-7)(t+1)=0,\quad t=-1,\frac73'),paragraph('The object will stop at 7/3 seconds, since we want time t ≥ 0.'),
 note('In Example 1(b), the original function and both derivatives exclude x = 1/3. In Example 2, the zero velocity is an instantaneous stop: for t ≥ 0, velocity is negative before 7/3 and positive afterward, so the model describes a reversal of direction.')
]),page('056_page_1.jpg',103,[
 heading('4.1.1 Higher order derivatives of algebraic, trigonometric, exponential and logarithmic functions'),paragraph('The successive derivatives of some functions are gathered to obtain the general form of nth derivatives in the following cases:'),
 heading('i. The nth derivative of f(x) = (ax + b)ᵐ'),paragraph('If f(x) = (ax + b)ᵐ, m is positive integer, then the successive derivatives of the given function developed a general term for the nth derivative of a function:'),
 eq(r'f(x)=(ax+b)^m'),eq(r'f′(x)=ma(ax+b)^{m-1}'),eq(r'f″(x)=m(m-1)a^2(ax+b)^{m-2}'),
 eq(r'f^{(n)}(x)=m(m-1)(m-2)...(m-n+1)a^n(ax+b)^{m-n}','i'),eq(r'=\frac{m!}{(m-n)!}a^n(ax+b)^{m-n}\quad\text{if }m\text{ is positive integer}'),
 paragraph(r'If m = −1, then the nth derivative of \(f(x)=\frac1{ax+b}\) is obtained by inserting m = −1 in equation (i):'),
 eq(r'f^{(n)}(x)=(-1)(-2)(-3)...(-n)a^n(ax+b)^{-1-n}=\frac{(-1)^nn!a^n}{(ax+b)^{n+1}}','ii'),
 heading('ii. The nth derivative of f(x) = ln(ax + b)'),paragraph('If f(x) = ln(ax + b), then the successive derivatives developed a general term for the nth derivative of a function:'),
 eq(r'f(x)=\ln(ax+b)'),eq(r'f′(x)=\frac a{ax+b}'),eq(r'f″(x)=\frac{(-1)a^2}{(ax+b)^2}'),eq(r'f‴(x)=\frac{(-1)(-2)a^3}{(ax+b)^3}'),
 eq(r'f^{(n)}(x)=(-1)(-2)(-3)...[-(n-1)]a^n(ax+b)^{-n}=\frac{(-1)^{n-1}(n-1)!a^n}{(ax+b)^n}','iii'),
 heading('iii. The nth derivative of f(x) = aᵐˣ'),paragraph('If f(x) = aᵐˣ, then the successive derivatives developed a general term for the nth derivative:'),
 eq(r'f(x)=a^{mx}'),eq(r'f′(x)=a^{mx}\log_ea\frac{d}{dx}(mx)=ma^{mx}\log_ea'),
 eq(r'f″(x)=m\log_ea\frac{d}{dx}(a^{mx})=m\log_ea\left[a^{mx}\log_ea\frac{d}{dx}(mx)\right]','iv'),
 note('Here n is a positive integer. For a polynomial degree m, the factorial formula applies for n ≤ m; when n > m, the derivative is zero and (m − n)! is not defined. The reciprocal formula requires ax + b ≠ 0; the real logarithm requires ax + b > 0. The real exponential requires a > 0, with m constant. The exponential derivation continues on page 104.')
]),page('056_page_2.jpg',104,[
 heading('Exponential nth derivative — continued'),eq(r'=m^2a^{mx}(\log_ea)^2'),eq(r'f^{(n)}(x)=m^na^{mx}(\log_ea)^n'),paragraph(r'If a = e, then the nth derivative of \(f(x)=e^{mx}\) is obtained by inserting a = e:'),
 eq(r'f(x)=e^{mx}'),eq(r'f′(x)=me^{mx}'),eq(r'f″(x)=m(me^{mx})=m^2e^{mx}'),eq(r'f^{(n)}(x)=m^ne^{mx}','v'),
 heading('iv. The nth derivative of f(x) = sin(ax + b)'),paragraph('If f(x) = sin(ax + b), then the successive derivatives developed a general term for the nth derivative of a function:'),
 eq(r'f(x)=\sin(ax+b)'),eq(r'f′(x)=a\cos(ax+b)=a\sin(ax+b+\frac{\pi}{2})'),
 eq(r'f″(x)=a^2\cos(ax+b+\frac{\pi}{2})=a^2\sin(ax+b+\frac{2\pi}2)'),
 eq(r'f‴(x)=a^2\cos(ax+b+\frac{2\pi}2)=a^3\sin(ax+b+\frac{3\pi}2)'),
 eq(r'f^{(n)}(x)=a^n\sin(ax+b+\frac{n\pi}2)','vi'),
 note('The printed intermediate cos term in the third derivative has coefficient a². Differentiating the second derivative multiplies by a again, so this coefficient should be a³; the final sine term and general nth-derivative formula are correct.'),
 heading('v. The nth derivative of f(x) = cos(ax + b)'),paragraph('If f(x) = cos(ax + b), then the successive derivatives developed a general term for the n-th derivative of a function:'),
 eq(r'f(x)=\cos(ax+b)'),eq(r'f′(x)=-a\sin(ax+b)=a\cos(ax+b+\frac{\pi}{2})'),
 eq(r'f″(x)=-a^2\sin(ax+b+\frac{\pi}{2})=a^2\cos(ax+b+\frac{2\pi}2)'),
 eq(r'f‴(x)=-a^3\sin(ax+b+\frac{2\pi}2)=a^3\cos(ax+b+\frac{3\pi}2)'),eq(r'f^{(n)}(x)=a^n\cos(ax+b+\frac{n\pi}2)','vii'),
 heading('Example 3'),paragraph(r'Find the 5th derivatives of the following functions: (a) \(f(x)=(6x+4)^9\); (b) \(f(x)=\frac1{4x+3}\); (c) \(f(x)=\ln(4x+7)\); (d) \(f(x)=6^{4x}\); (e) \(f(x)=e^{4x}\); (f) \(f(x)=\sin(5x+7)\).'),paragraph('The source solutions continue on page 105.')
]),page('057_page_1.jpg',105,[
 heading('Example 3 — Solution'),
 paragraph('a. If f(x) = (6x + 4)⁹ with a = 6, b = 4 and m = 9, then the 5th derivative of the given function is obtained by inserting n = 5 in equation:'),eq(r'f^{(n)}(x)=\frac{m!}{(m-n)!}a^n(ax+b)^{m-n}'),
 eq(r'f^{(5)}(x)=\frac{9!}{(9-5)!}6^5(6x+4)^{9-5}=\frac{6^5\,9!}{4!}(6x+4)^4=6^5(9)(8)(7)(6)(5)(6x+4)^4=6^5[15120(6x+4)^4]'),
 paragraph(r'b. If \(f(x)=\frac1{4x+3}\) with a = 4 and b = 3, then the 5th derivative of the given function is obtained by inserting n = 5 in equation:'),eq(r'f^{(n)}(x)=\frac{(-1)^nn!a^n}{(ax+b)^{n+1}}'),eq(r'f^{(5)}(x)=\frac{(-1)^5\,5!4^5}{(4x+3)^6}=\frac{-4^5\,5!}{(4x+3)^6}'),
 paragraph('c. If f(x) = ln(4x + 7) with a = 4 and b = 7, then the 5th derivative of the given function is obtained by inserting n = 5 in equation:'),eq(r'f^{(n)}(x)=\frac{(-1)^{n-1}(n-1)!a^n}{(ax+b)^n}'),eq(r'f^{(5)}(x)=\frac{(-1)^4\,4!4^5}{(4x+7)^5}=\frac{4^5\,4!}{(4x+7)^5}'),
 paragraph('d. If f(x) = 6⁴ˣ with a = 6 and m = 4, then the 5th derivative of the given function is obtained by inserting n = 5 in equation:'),eq(r'f^{(n)}(x)=m^na^{mx}(\log_ea)^n'),eq(r'f^{(5)}(x)=4^5\,6^{4x}(\log6)^5'),
 paragraph('e. If f(x) = e⁴ˣ with m = 4, then the 5th derivative of the given function is obtained by inserting n = 5 in equation:'),eq(r'f^{(n)}(x)=m^ne^{mx}'),eq(r'f^{(5)}(x)=4^5e^{4x}'),
 paragraph('f. If f(x) = sin(5x + 7) with a = 5 and b = 7, then the 5th derivative of the given function is obtained by inserting n = 5 in equation:'),eq(r'f^{(n)}(x)=a^n\sin(ax+b+\frac{n\pi}2)'),eq(r'f^{(5)}(x)=5^5\sin(5x+7+\frac{5\pi}2)'),
 heading('4.1.2 Second derivative of implicit, inverse trigonometric and parametric functions'),
 heading('Example 4'),paragraph(r'Find the second derivative of \(x^2y+2y^3=3x+2y\).'),heading('Solution'),paragraph(r'The equation is \(x^2y+2y^3=3x+2y\).'),eq(r'x^2y+2y^3=3x+2y','i'),paragraph('The first implicit derivative of (i) w.r.t. x is:'),
 eq(r'\frac{d}{dx}(x^2y+2y^3)=\frac{d}{dx}(3x+2y)'),eq(r'\frac{d}{dx}(x^2y)+\frac{d}{dx}(2y^3)=\frac{d}{dx}(3x)+\frac{d}{dx}(2y)'),eq(r'2xy+x^2\frac{dy}{dx}+6y^2\frac{dy}{dx}=3+2\frac{dy}{dx}'),
 note('The source second-derivative calculation continues on page 106. Example 3(b) excludes x = −3/4; Example 3(c) requires x > −7/4. In Example 3(d), log6 means the natural logarithm logₑ6 from its governing formula, rather than a common logarithm.')
])]
