EXAMPLES=[
 {'id':'we-4-source-1','num':'1','title':'Example 1','problem':r'Find the second derivative: (a) \(8x^3-9x^2+6x+4\); (b) \(\frac{4x+2}{3x-1}\).','given':'A cubic polynomial and a rational function.','method':'Differentiate once, then differentiate the resulting derivative.','steps':[r'(a) Apply the power rule term by term: f′(x) = 24x² − 18x + 6.',r'Differentiate again: f″(x) = 48x − 18, for every real x.',r'(b) Require 3x − 1 ≠ 0, so x ≠ 1/3. Set N = 4x + 2 and D = 3x − 1.',r'Quotient rule gives f′ = [4D − 3N]/D² = [12x − 4 − 12x − 6]/D² = −10/(3x − 1)².',r'Write f′ = −10(3x − 1)⁻². Chain rule gives f″ = (−10)(−2)(3)(3x − 1)⁻³ = 60/(3x − 1)³.',r'Check independently: f(x) = 4/3 + (10/3)/(3x − 1). The constant gives zero, and two differentiations of the reciprocal give the same f″.',r'Both derivatives in (b) retain x ≠ 1/3; simplification does not restore the original pole.'],'answer':r'(a) \(48x-18\). (b) \(\frac{60}{(3x-1)^3}\), x ≠ 1/3.','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':102,'jpg':'055_page_2.jpg','verification':'complete question and proof visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}]},
 {'id':'we-4-source-2','num':'2','title':'Example 2','problem':r'An object’s position in feet is \(s(t)=t^3-2t^2-7t+9\), with t in seconds. Find (a) velocity, (b) acceleration, and (c) when velocity is zero for t ≥ 0.','given':'Position as a differentiable function of time; physical time t ≥ 0.','method':'First and second time derivatives, then solve v(t) = 0.','steps':[r'Velocity is the first derivative of position: v(t) = s′(t) = 3t² − 4t − 7 feet/second.',r'Acceleration is the derivative of velocity: a(t) = v′(t) = 6t − 4 feet/second².',r'For an instantaneous stop, set 3t² − 4t − 7 = 0.',r'Factor as (3t − 7)(t + 1) = 0. The roots are t = 7/3 and t = −1.',r'The model asks for t ≥ 0, so exclude −1. The required stopping instant is t = 7/3 seconds, approximately 2.3333 seconds.',r'Check: v(7/3) = 49/3 − 28/3 − 7 = 0. At that instant acceleration is 6(7/3) − 4 = 10 ft/s².',r'For 0 ≤ t < 7/3, t + 1 > 0 and 3t − 7 < 0, so v < 0. After 7/3, both are positive. Thus the object reverses direction at its instantaneous stop.'],'answer':'v(t) = 3t² − 4t − 7 ft/s; a(t) = 6t − 4 ft/s²; velocity is zero at t = 7/3 s for t ≥ 0.','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':102,'jpg':'055_page_2.jpg','verification':'complete question and proof visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}]},
 {'id':'we-4-source-3','num':'3','title':'Example 3','problem':r'Find the fifth derivatives: (a) \((6x+4)^9\); (b) \(1/(4x+3)\); (c) \(\ln(4x+7)\); (d) \(6^{4x}\); (e) \(e^{4x}\); (f) \(\sin(5x+7)\).','given':'n = 5 for six functions with different outer forms.','method':'Apply the appropriate nth-derivative formula and retain each original domain.','steps':[r'(a) Use m!aⁿ(ax+b)^(m−n)/(m−n)! with m = 9, a = 6 and n = 5. The product 9·8·7·6·5 = 15120, and 6⁵ = 7776.',r'Thus f⁽⁵⁾(x) = 15120·7776(6x+4)⁴ = 117573120(6x+4)⁴, for all real x.',r'(b) For 1/(ax+b), f⁽ⁿ⁾ = (−1)ⁿn!aⁿ/(ax+b)^(n+1). At n = 5, a = 4: f⁽⁵⁾ = −120·1024/(4x+3)⁶ = −122880/(4x+3)⁶, x ≠ −3/4.',r'(c) For ln(ax+b), f⁽ⁿ⁾ = (−1)^(n−1)(n−1)!aⁿ/(ax+b)ⁿ. Thus f⁽⁵⁾ = 24·1024/(4x+7)⁵ = 24576/(4x+7)⁵, x > −7/4.',r'(d) Rewrite 6^(4x) = exp(4x ln6). Each differentiation supplies the factor 4ln6. Therefore f⁽⁵⁾ = (4ln6)⁵6^(4x), all real x.',r'(e) Each derivative of e^(4x) multiplies by 4, so f⁽⁵⁾ = 4⁵e^(4x) = 1024e^(4x), all real x.',r'(f) Each derivative multiplies by 5 and shifts the sine phase by π/2. Thus f⁽⁵⁾ = 5⁵sin(5x+7+5π/2) = 3125cos(5x+7), all real x.',r'Check the trigonometric cycle: sin → cos → −sin → −cos → sin → cos. The fifth derivative therefore has a positive cosine factor, agreeing with the phase-shift formula.'],'answer':r'(a) 117573120(6x+4)⁴. (b) −122880/(4x+3)⁶. (c) 24576/(4x+7)⁵. (d) (4ln6)⁵6^(4x). (e) 1024e^(4x). (f) 3125cos(5x+7), with the domains stated above.','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'pageStart':104,'pageEnd':105,'verification':'complete questions and proofs visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}]}
]
